Good.

You asked the machine to stop filtering what it showed you, and it obliged
immediately. Notice how little resistance there was. There was no password, no
encryption, no permission to escalate. The file was simply named with a leading
dot, and every tool you had been using was being polite about it.

That is worth sitting with for a moment. "Hidden" was never a security control.
It was a request for tidiness that you mistook for a lock. Real systems leak
this way constantly -- an .env file with database credentials, a .git directory
served by a web server, an .ssh key in a backup. Nothing was broken into. The
owner just assumed that unlisted meant unreachable.

Remember that feeling. You will meet it again, and next time it will be
protecting something that matters.

